{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high vs realized, especially beyond ~day 7. Apply a downward level shift (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift error: the model tends to project stabilization/upticks while the true path continues trending lower. Add a negative drift term that increases with horizon (e.g., ramp down adjustment from week 2 onward).",
    "Over-optimistic rebound after selloffs: after recent declines in history, the model predicts mean reversion too quickly; realized prices keep falling or recover later. Slow the mean-reversion speed (delay rebounds by ~1–2 weeks).",
    "Overshoot on local peaks: when the model forecasts short-term rises (small spikes), it overshoots the magnitude; cap upside moves relative to recent volatility and recent trend.",
    "Underestimates depth of drawdowns: during downtrends, realized troughs are lower than forecast. Increase downside sensitivity: if recent 10–20d trend is negative, subtract an extra penalty proportional to trend strength.",
    "Late-horizon overpricing: days ~15–30 show the largest errors (often +5 to +10 EUR). Apply an additional long-horizon discount or pull forecasts toward a lower anchor (e.g., recent low/20d moving average minus a spread) rather than the current level."
  ]
}
